; The network lock's priority ceiling (sys/network/ip/ether.lisp,
; WITH-NETWORK-LOCK; QUUX's Ethernet contract, section 5.1, senders;
; lisp/network-lock-ceiling.lisp): a sender runs at the network process's
; priority, 25, while it holds the lock, raised before it takes it and
; restored after it releases it, at the outermost hold only, in an
; UNWIND-PROTECT.  The cases run in the TELNET server's listener, at priority
; 0, the sections in the check's compiled functions.  On a band built before
; the change, compile ether.lisp first (README.md).  Without the ceiling the
; priority inside is the listener's own, 0; a ceiling that never restores
; leaves the listener at 25 after the first section.

; the listener's own priority, raised inside and restored after: (BEFORE INSIDE AFTER)
(list (send current-process :priority) (nlc-inside) (send current-process :priority)) => (0 25 0)
; the lock the listener's inside, free after
(list (nlc-holder) ip::network-lock) => (T NIL)
; nested holds: raised once, restored once, at the outermost
(list (nlc-nested) (send current-process :priority)) => (25 0)
(nlc-nested-around) => (25 INNER 25)
; a throw and an error out of the section: the lock released, the priority restored
(progn (nlc-throw) (list (send current-process :priority) ip::network-lock)) => (0 NIL)
(progn (nlc-error) (list (send current-process :priority) ip::network-lock)) => (0 NIL)
; a process above the ceiling keeps its own priority throughout
(nlc-above 30) => (30 30 30)
; the section's values come back
(nlc-values) => (1 2 3)
; the holder's entry in si:active-processes, moved by the raise and the
; restore themselves: the list stays in priority order (before, inside, after a
; section); inside, the entry is where :set-priority to 25 puts it; after, it
; is back at its place before
(nlc-order) => (T T T)
(nlc-places) => (T T)
; a section that moves its own entry (:set-priority 30 inside): the restore
; walks instead, and the priority and the order come back
(nlc-fallback) => (0 T)
